{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And I saw, and behold, you had sinned.</b> What did he see? He saw that the writing had flown off them, and therefore: ״ואתפוש בשני הלוחות ואשברם לעיניכם״. A parable: to a courier who was walking on the road with the king's seal in his hand. He came to enter the city, passed through the river, and the seal fell into the water and the writing was erased. What did he do? He tore the paper and broke the seal. So too Moses — when he was descending from the mountain and the tablets were in his hand, the writing flew off them and he broke them." "Another interpretation: <b>And I saw</b> — he saw the calf that they had made and broke them. A parable: to a king who betrothed a woman and said to her, 'After a time I will send your ketubbah in the hand of the shushvin.' While the shushvin was coming and the ketubbah was in his hand, he found that she had spoiled and committed adultery. The shushvin saw the wrongdoing and tore the ketubbah. He said: Better she should be judged as an unmarried woman and not as a married woman. Thus the Holy One, blessed be He, betrothed Israel at the giving of the Torah and said to send the tablets — which are the ketubbah — within forty days, by the hand of Moses the shushvin. Moses came and the ketubbah was in his hand, and he saw that they had spoiled with the calf, and he tore the ketubbah."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:00:41.983338577Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}